	doi = {10.1038/nrendo.2009.103},
	abstract = {Noninvasive imaging and quantification of pancreatic, insulin‑producing β cells has been considered a high‑priority field of investigation for the past decade. in the first review on this issue, attention was already paid to various agents for labeling β cells, including 6‑125i‑d‑glucose, 65Zn, 3H‑glibenclamide, 3H‑mitiglinide, an 125i‑labeled mouse monoclonal antibody against β‑cell surface ganglioside(s), d‑(U‑14C)‑glucose and 2‑deoxy‑2‑18F‑d‑glucose to label glycogen accumulated in β cells in response to sustained hyperglycemia, and, last but not least, an analog of d‑mannoheptulose. This review discusses these methods and further contributions. For instance, emphasis is placed on labeling β cells with 11C‑dihydrotetrabenazine, which is the most advanced method at present. Attention is also drawn to the latest approaches for noninvasive imaging and functional characterization of pancreatic β cells. None of these procedures is used in clinical practice yet.},
